  replace(
    content,
    'At the first rest stop, Ayel sat down in the grass.</p><p>"Sam."</p><p>"Yes."</p><p>"I am going to talk now and I want you to let me."</p><p>"I will let you."</p><p>"I am carrying a child. I have been carrying the child for two or three weeks and I did not know. The child was conceived at the same time Aresia''s was conceived, more or less, which means it was conceived in the first few days of the encampment stay. Which means Theron. I am being specific about the window because I want to be specific about what the child is going to be. The child will be half me and half Theron. The child will be a Hjord-Beambreak child, which is a combination not much seen north of here in my memory. The child will be raised in Beambreak by my mother and me. It will visit Hjord territory when the road is safe enough and when the child is old enough. It will learn the Hjord songs by listening to the letters Theron will send. That is the shape of the child. I have been figuring it out in the last hour while we walked and I have the shape now."</p><p>"Ayel."</p><p>"Yes."</p><p>"You have had the shape of a great many things very quickly today."</p><p>"I have."</p><p>"Are you all right."</p><p>"I am not all right. I am <em>functional</em>. The word is doing more work today than it normally does. I have been functional for about an hour and I am going to be functional for the next several hours and at some point tonight at the fire I am going to cry about this in the proper way that the not-being-all-right requires, and you are going to sit beside me and not try to make it better. Will you do that."</p><p>"I will do that."</p><p>"Good."</p><p>"Ayel."</p><p>"Yes."</p><p>"Whatever you need. At any point on this road. You tell me. That is the rule you set two weeks ago and it is not only yours. It is ours. I will follow it for my changes and you will follow it for yours. If your body needs us to stop for a day, we stop. If your body needs us to walk faster to get out of this stretch of the plains before dark, we walk faster. You set the pace for the next nine months. I carry whatever you tell me to carry."</p><p>"Sam."</p><p>"Yes."</p><p>"That is a good sentence."</p><p>"I have been saving it."</p><p>She laughed again. The laugh was a little more settled this time — not happy, but <em>landed</em>, the laugh of a woman who had been given a heavy fact and had begun, in the walking and the talking and the Sam-beside-her, to figure out where to put it down.</p><p>She reached out and took his hand.</p><p>The <em>thélia</em> climbed into her lap.</p><p>They sat together for a few more minutes.',
    'At the first rest stop, Ayel sat down in the grass.</p><p>"Sam."</p><p>"Yes."</p><p>"My legs are complaining."</p><p>"They have not had a proper walking day in a month."</p><p>"They have not. I have been riding <em>agéli</em> and sitting in tents and walking the encampment perimeter and that is not the same as eight hours of road."</p><p>"You will recover by tomorrow."</p><p>"I know I will. I am registering the complaint now because registering it is the only concession my legs are going to get. Also I want water."</p><p>He handed her the waterskin. She drank. She passed it back. She lay flat on her back in the grass with her hands folded on her stomach and her eyes half-closed against the brightness of the noon sky.</p><p>"Sam."</p><p>"Yes."</p><p>"That was a good month."</p><p>"It was."</p><p>"I am going to think about it at odd intervals for the rest of my life."</p><p>"That is the shape of a good month."</p><p>"It is."</p><p>She lay in the grass for a few more minutes. The <em>thélia</em> investigated a beetle. Sam stood and watched the horizon and did not interrupt. When she was ready she got up on her own, and they kept walking, and the walking was the rhythm of two people who had work ahead of them and had agreed without saying it that the best use of the road between here and there was to cover it.'
  ),
  '"Not just the work."</p><p>"No. Not just the work. I am also bringing — I am bringing a different relationship with the idea of fathering. I had not considered myself a potential father before this month. I had not put it on any list. I have now fathered four children inside the wombs of four women, none of whom I had met a month ago, and I am going to be a returner to those children for the rest of the first decade of their lives, and the returner-function is going to be a part of me I did not know I was going to have. I am bringing that home. I am bringing the knowledge that I can do that. That is a bigger thing than it sounds."</p><p>"It is a big thing."</p><p>"It is. And I am bringing the frames. And I am bringing your child."</p><p>"My child is mine."</p><p>"Your child is ours, in the particular sense that I travel with you and I care about what is between us and I care about what is between you and Theron and I care about the child that is now on the road with us. The child is yours. I am not claiming it as mine. I am saying I am not separate from it. I will carry the knowledge of it. I will help you carry the child whenever carrying help is useful. I will be — I will be uncle to it, or something like uncle, in the Beambreak structure of things. The structure will figure itself out."</p><p>"Uncle is a good word."</p><p>"Is it."</p><p>"It is. I was worried you were going to say <em>second father</em> or something, and I was going to have to correct you. Uncle is — uncle is correct. Thank you for finding the word."</p><p>"You are welcome."',
  '"Not just the work."</p><p>"No. Not just the work. I am also bringing — I am bringing a different relationship with the idea of fathering. I had not considered myself a potential father before this month. I had not put it on any list. I have now fathered four children inside the wombs of four women, none of whom I had met a month ago, and I am going to be a returner to those children for the rest of the first decade of their lives, and the returner-function is going to be a part of me I did not know I was going to have. I am bringing that home. I am bringing the knowledge that I can do that. That is a bigger thing than it sounds."</p><p>"It is a big thing."</p><p>"It is."</p><p>"What else."</p><p>"A language I did not speak a month ago. I have about two hundred Hjord words now. They arrived faster than I expected. I think it is because the frames I carry are tuned to a people who speak the language, and the frames are giving me the grammar by a route that is not the usual route. I do not know how deep the giving goes. I will find out when I try to read something complicated."</p><p>"That is useful."</p><p>"It is useful. I am also bringing a body that is sixteen pounds heavier than it was a month ago and that hums when a person puts their ear against it, and I do not yet understand either of those things fully, but I am carrying both of them home, so whatever they are going to be they are going to be in Beambreak."</p><p>"You have had a productive month."</p><p>"I have. What else are you bringing."</p><p>"Aside from the common project."</p><p>"Aside from that."</p><p>"I am bringing an appreciation for how much faster a decision gets made in a herd than it does in a court. I am bringing a specific set of herbs Lyra pressed on me that I do not have the names of in Beambreak terms but that I am going to learn the names of and bring into the Beambreak pharmacy. I am bringing the scout-shape, which I did not have before the month and which I am going to practise until I can hold it at a raptor level. And I am bringing the memory of a month that did not have a court in it, which is a memory I have never had access to before, and which I am going to keep for the specific purpose of reminding myself — when the court is being the court — that there are other ways for a population to be."</p><p>"That is a useful memory to keep."</p><p>"It is."'
),
